WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.400
Why are people pausing "The Lord of&nbsp;
the Rings" to watch someone break a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.240
toe or get their finger bitten&nbsp;
off? Keep watching to find out!

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:14.640
As the town that serves as home to Frodo and&nbsp;
Bilbo Baggins, Hobbiton is the quintessential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:20.640
ideal of a quiet locale where any hobbit would&nbsp;
love to live. Author J.R.R. Tolkien based the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.520
fictional village on the beauty and tranquility of&nbsp;
the English countryside, and it's where the tale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:31.200
truly begins. The description of Hobbiton in the&nbsp;
novels not only gives readers a sense of what life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:36.720
is like for a hobbit, but has made generations of&nbsp;
fans yearn for their own cozy little hobbit hole.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:42.560
Peter Jackson — a superfan of the novels — and the&nbsp;
rest of "The Lord of the Rings" crew knew they had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:47.600
to find the perfect location to film the Hobbiton&nbsp;
scenes, and they found what they were looking for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:54.560
in New Zealand. According to the Hobbiton Tours&nbsp;
website, a 1,250-acre farm would eventually become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:59.360
the Shire. The website also states that it took&nbsp;
over nine months of construction to build the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:04.480
hobbit village, with the film crew actually&nbsp;
receiving help from the New Zealand Army.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:09.280
The result of the filmmakers' hard work paid off&nbsp;
in the final cut of the film. At the beginning of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:13.840
"The Fellowship of the Ring," we see Frodo and&nbsp;
Gandalf riding in a carriage down the pastoral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:19.120
streets of Hobbiton, and for those who've read the&nbsp;
books, it feels like the pages of the novels come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:25.040
to life. In 2002, guided tours were made available&nbsp;
for anyone to come and visit the set, and after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:31.200
Jackson rebuilt the set in 2009 for "The Hobbit,"&nbsp;
the site became a permanent tourist destination.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:37.200
After his unforgettable 111th birthday&nbsp;
celebration, Bilbo makes preparations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.280
to leave Hobbiton for good. Although it&nbsp;
proves to be an immense struggle for him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:44.240
Bilbo manages to leave the&nbsp;
One Ring behind with Frodo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:49.280
along with most of his belongings and the deed&nbsp;
to his home, Bag End. At Gandalf's direction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:54.960
Frodo keeps the ring "secret" and "safe," even&nbsp;
though he has no idea of the true power it holds.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:56.800
Gandalf, having his suspicions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:00.560
leaves the Shire to find out what he&nbsp;
can about Bilbo's mysterious ring.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:06.320
When he returns, he is haggard and disturbed,&nbsp;
not the calm and collected wizard he once was.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:11.600
Frodo hands over the ring, only to be surprised&nbsp;
by Gandalf throwing it into the fireplace.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:16.720
What follows is, perhaps, one of the&nbsp;
most recognizable shots in the trilogy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:20.560
Gandalf takes the ring out of the&nbsp;
fire and places it in Frodo's hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:24.240
assuring the hobbit that it is&nbsp;
"quite cool." Moments later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:35.840
writing appears across the band, which Gandalf&nbsp;
ominously translates for Frodo and the audience:

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:37.225
"One ring to rule them all, one ring to find them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.225 --> 00:02:38.800
one ring to bring them all and&nbsp;
in the darkness bind them."

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:44.240
Not only is this phrase insanely quotable, but&nbsp;
it establishes what will become the central plot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:49.520
of the series. The One Ring of Power is an evil&nbsp;
artifact that must be destroyed at all costs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:54.960
and poor Frodo is the one to do it. Although&nbsp;
Gandalf hates sending his hobbit friend on such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:59.280
a dangerous errand, he hopes that Frodo's&nbsp;
bravery and loyalty will get the job done.

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:04.560
As Frodo begins his journey out of the&nbsp;
Shire, he is joined by his loyal friend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:10.240
Samwise Gamgee, and later by the mischievous&nbsp;
duo Pippin Took and Merry Brandybuck. Merry and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:14.880
Pippin literally bump into Sam and Frodo while&nbsp;
on the run from the crotchety Farmer Maggot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.200
after stealing some of his prized vegetables.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:21.840
After the four hobbits tumble down a hill, they&nbsp;
gather themselves to their feet just as Frodo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:26.960
senses a dangerous presence coming down the road.&nbsp;
Frodo frantically commands the others to hide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:30.560
and they find the perfect spot&nbsp;
under the roots of a large tree.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:34.320
The scene is one of the most suspenseful&nbsp;
moments in the movie, with the four hobbits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:39.040
huddled together in a dank hole teeming with&nbsp;
creepy-crawlies, as the terrifying figure of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:43.680
the hooded dark rider — one of the dreaded&nbsp;
Ringwraiths — approaches. As the Ringwraith&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:49.440
sniffs out his unseen prey, Frodo almost gives&nbsp;
into temptation and nearly puts on the One Ring.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:54.400
Luckily for our heroes, the rider is distracted&nbsp;
and passes without discovering their hiding place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:58.880
It's a wake-up call for the hobbits that there are&nbsp;
great dangers waiting for them outside the Shire.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:04.080
Although Tolkein's work is held in high esteem,&nbsp;
the unfortunate truth is that there is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:08.800
sad lack of female presence in his novels. To&nbsp;
make up for this, the writers of "The Lord of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:13.520
the Rings" film trilogy expanded the role of&nbsp;
an underutilized character from the novels:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:18.480
Arwen Evenstar. The writers gave&nbsp;
Arwen a much larger part in the films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:22.320
sometimes adding her into scenes from&nbsp;
which she was absent in the books.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:25.360
In "The Fellowship of the Ring,"&nbsp;
the hobbits' harrowing ordeal on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:30.080
Weathertop results in Frodo being stabbed by&nbsp;
a Morgul blade — which, if left untreated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:34.480
will turn Frodo into a shadow of&nbsp;
himself, not unlike the Ringwraiths.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:38.960
Luckily, Arwen [AR-wen] comes upon the group and&nbsp;
insists on taking Frodo to her father, Elrond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:43.600
to get healed before it's too late. What&nbsp;
follows is a tension-fueled chase scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:49.200
with Arwen riding as fast as she can with Frodo&nbsp;
in tow, and the Nazgul hot on their heels.

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:53.440
The chase comes to a climax when Arwen and&nbsp;
Frodo cross the river that forms the border&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:57.600
of the elven lands. In a show of great&nbsp;
courage, Arwen challenges the riders:

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:00.160
"If you want him, come and claim him!"

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:06.080
It's tough not to be impressed by Arwen's heroism&nbsp;
in that moment, especially for a character who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:11.360
was essentially wasted in the original novels.&nbsp;
She then conjures up a great wave to wash away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:15.760
the enemies. This scene is truly memorable —&nbsp;
not only for the impressive special effects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:19.120
but also for the way it establishes&nbsp;
Arwen's character in the films.

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:24.800
One does not simply rewatch the "council of&nbsp;
Elrond" scene in "The Fellowship of the Ring"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:28.800
without pausing at the exact moment&nbsp;
that Boromir makes his iconic pose.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:33.520
You know the one — he's looking very serious&nbsp;
and holding up his hand with his fingers forming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:38.000
a circle, indicating the Eye of Sauron.&nbsp;
Meanwhile, the audience is picturing their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:41.440
own fill-in-the-blank text&nbsp;
superimposed over the image.

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:46.160
In the very slim chance that you are in the&nbsp;
dark on this one, the "One Does Not Simply"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:51.520
meme was a popular fad that spammed the internet&nbsp;
for several years starting in 2004, in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:56.480
someone would start a phrase with "One does not&nbsp;
simply," and then finish the thought accordingly.

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:00.400
It's also refreshing to note that&nbsp;
the actor himself, Sean Bean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:02.880
is very aware of the meme&nbsp;
and finds it quite amusing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:08.400
In fact, he once told Radio Times magazine that he&nbsp;
thinks the memes featuring his face will be his,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:12.320
quote, "unintended legacy," and he&nbsp;
seems to be totally on board with it.

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:18.080
As the wizard to end all wizards, Gandalf&nbsp;
the Grey is the epitome of wisdom, power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:23.440
and kindness. He acts as a guide and mentor to&nbsp;
those who follow him, yet he is also a loyal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:28.240
friend, and a fearsome fighter that any&nbsp;
good guy is lucky to have on their side.

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:33.120
Perhaps the most spectacular scene in "The&nbsp;
Fellowship of the Ring" is Gandalf's face-off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:37.280
against the Balrog in the Mines of Moria.&nbsp;
Not only are the special effects for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:47.840
creature itself awe-inspiring, but we get to&nbsp;
see Gandalf looking like an incredible badass.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:48.000
"You shall not pass!"

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:51.600
Just as we cheer for Gandalf while&nbsp;
he holds back the monstrous foe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:56.320
so we weep for him when he falls into the&nbsp;
depths below. No one can forget the image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:01.440
of Gandalf clinging to the edge of the rock&nbsp;
before his final counsel for the fellowship:

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:02.480
"Fly, you fools!"

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:08.320
Even those who knew Gandalf would return in the&nbsp;
second film couldn't help but feel something akin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:14.000
to grief at his loss. For the ones for whom&nbsp;
Gandalf's fall came as a complete surprise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:18.240
you probably had to press pause on your remote&nbsp;
to ask yourself what the heck just happened.

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:22.480
Though he mostly appears in the first&nbsp;
film of "The Lord of the Rings" trilogy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:26.240
the character of Boromir had a surprising&nbsp;
impact on the series as a whole.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:31.440
Part of that is likely due to the superb&nbsp;
performance of actor Sean Bean, yet it can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:36.000
also be attributed to the writing team's careful&nbsp;
consideration of the character's development.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:41.440
It could easily be misconstrued that Boromir is&nbsp;
a bad guy, when in fact he is much more complex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:46.000
than that. In truth, he is a man desperate&nbsp;
to save his people, and that desperation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:51.760
is a weakness that the One Ring seeks to exploit.&nbsp;
When Boromir tries to take the ring from Frodo by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:56.160
force in the third act of "The Fellowship of&nbsp;
the Ring," he immediately recognizes his error&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.160 --> 00:08:00.880
and attempts to compensate by doing what&nbsp;
he was meant to do: protect the innocent.

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:06.800
Sadly, Boromir finds death in a hail of arrows&nbsp;
while defending Merry and Pippin during an attack.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:11.680
With his dying words, he confesses his faults&nbsp;
to Aragorn, while pledging his final allegiance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:16.400
to the true king of Gondor. The heartbreaking&nbsp;
scene is enough to bring a tear to anyone's eye,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:25.840
and might have you reaching for the&nbsp;
pause button so you can compose yourself.

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:28.998
"I would have followed you, my&nbsp;
brother. My captain. My King!"

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:32.400
In "The Two Towers," Aragorn, Legolas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:37.280
and Gimli are tracking a pack of orcs who have&nbsp;
kidnapped their friends, Merry and Pippin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:42.560
During their search, they come upon a group of&nbsp;
soldiers led by Eomer. Eomer informs the trio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:46.960
that he and his soldiers recently ambushed&nbsp;
a group of orcs, but left no survivors.

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:51.840
When Aragorn and the others find the remains&nbsp;
of the orc horde, they find no trace of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:56.560
hobbit companions. Believing the halflings&nbsp;
may be dead, Aragorn lashes out by kicking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.560 --> 00:09:01.680
a nearby orc helmet and screaming as he falls&nbsp;
to his knees. While the kick was in the script,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:06.960
the scream was not, and that's because actor&nbsp;
Viggo Mortensen broke his toe in that take.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:11.840
Yet instead of asking for the director to cut,&nbsp;
Mortensen used his physical pain to express&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:15.520
the torment his character was feeling&nbsp;
in the moment. Talk about method acting!

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:21.600
One of the most beloved characters in the trilogy,&nbsp;
Gollum is a creature somehow both detestable and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:26.480
highly sympathetic. After being in possession of&nbsp;
the One Ring for many years, the hobbit-like being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:33.200
once known as Smeagol has become an emaciated husk&nbsp;
who lives only to hold his "precious" once more.

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:36.400
Under layers of hatred and greed,&nbsp;
though, there is still the person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:41.520
who was once Smeagol, and Frodo's compassion&nbsp;
allows Smeagol to appear through the cracks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:47.280
Where Gollum is suspicious and filled with rancor,&nbsp;
Smeagol is trusting and eager to please. In one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:52.160
the most memorable Gollum scenes, we see the two&nbsp;
sides of the character arguing with one another as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:56.080
if they were two separate personalities&nbsp;
— and indeed, in many ways, they are.

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:01.200
The scene was shot brilliantly by director&nbsp;
Jackson in a two-camera setup, with motion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:06.880
capture performer Andy Serkis giving a magnificent&nbsp;
depiction of the dual nature of the character.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:11.360
The audience really feels as if they are&nbsp;
seeing a conversation between two people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:14.720
and it's a scene worth pausing to&nbsp;
take in the sheer genius of it all.

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:20.640
The monumental Battle of Helm's Deep is&nbsp;
inarguably the highlight of "The Two Towers."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:24.960
Taking up 39 minutes of the movie's total&nbsp;
three-hour runtime, there are plenty of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:29.200
moments in the action-packed sequence for&nbsp;
audiences to focus on. However, there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:34.240
one bit in particular that not only adds to the&nbsp;
excitement, but gets a big laugh from viewers.

00:10:34.240 --> 00:10:38.400
You may remember the escape in "The Fellowship&nbsp;
of the Ring," in which our heroes run for their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:42.960
lives in the ruins of Moria. At one point,&nbsp;
Aragorn helps Sam get across a crumbling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:47.680
staircase by throwing him through the air&nbsp;
to be caught by Boromir on the other side.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:53.040
However, Gimli proudly refuses the ranger's&nbsp;
help, firmly stating: "Nobody tosses a dwarf!"

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:56.880
During the peak of the Helm's Deep battle,&nbsp;
Gimli and Aragorn make their way to a bridge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:00.240
overflowing with enemy soldiers&nbsp;
trying to break into the fortress.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:03.680
To get across the gap between the&nbsp;
bridge and the stairs at the outer wall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:08.880
Gimli reluctantly offers the only solution.&nbsp;
Under his breath, he tells Aragorn that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:13.600
ranger will have to toss him across the&nbsp;
gap to get to the bridge. Aragorn agrees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:18.960
although the dwarf first makes him promise not to&nbsp;
tell Legolas. He does have his pride, after all.

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:21.520
Gimli's stocky figure being&nbsp;
thrown through the air,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:26.560
landing triumphantly and hacking away at the&nbsp;
orcs is not only an awesome sight to see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:31.120
but a hilarious callback to the first film's&nbsp;
joke that's definitely worth watching again.

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:32.160
Pippin sings for Denethor

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:34.160
Throughout the first two&nbsp;
"Lord of the Rings" films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:38.160
the hobbits Merry and Pippin are rarely&nbsp;
seen apart — which made it all the more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:42.080
shocking when the two were separated in&nbsp;
the third entry, "The Return of the King."

00:11:42.080 --> 00:11:46.320
After Pippin makes the mistake of revealing&nbsp;
himself to Sauron through the mystical Palantir,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:50.160
Gandalf takes him to Minas Tirith,&nbsp;
the capital city of Gondor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:55.280
There, Pippin swears himself into the&nbsp;
service of the city's steward, Denethor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.280 --> 00:11:59.760
Though his hope is that he might make up for&nbsp;
his grave mistake by doing something noble,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:04.880
Pippin finds that Denethor is not the ideal&nbsp;
mentor. A man filled with bitterness over the loss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:09.840
of his son Boromir, Denethor would rather give&nbsp;
into despair than fight for the hope of mankind.

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:15.440
As the soldiers of Gondor go off to battle,&nbsp;
Denethor skulks in his grand hall with Pippin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:20.560
at his side. The steward requests that Pippin&nbsp;
sing a song for him, and though the hobbit is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:25.360
clearly not in the mood for a lively tune, he&nbsp;
obliges. As Pippin's voice echoes through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.360 --> 00:12:30.160
halls, we see the men of Gondor ride into a&nbsp;
hopeless battle that will seal their doom.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.160 --> 00:12:35.200
It's a truly haunting moment, and Boyd's beautiful&nbsp;
vocals are the perfect addition to the scene.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:39.920
The song he sings is one of sorrow and&nbsp;
grief, and it will never leave your head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:43.920
(sadly, neither will the disturbing image&nbsp;
of Denethor biting into a cherry tomato).

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:48.960
Though Helm's Deep is one of the most memorable&nbsp;
battle sequences in "The Lord of the Rings"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:53.440
trilogy, the Battle of Pelennor Fields from&nbsp;
"The Return of the King" perhaps holds the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:58.000
significance. It turns the tide of the War&nbsp;
of the Ring, and gives the good people of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:02.480
Middle-earth the upper hand against Sauron's&nbsp;
forces. While there are plenty of triumphant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:05.840
moments during this epic battle, there&nbsp;
is one that stands out above the rest.

00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:11.280
Eowyn, the shield-maiden of Rohan, has&nbsp;
dealt with misogyny all of her life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:15.520
While she is expected to be happy in&nbsp;
her gilded cage at Theoden's court,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:19.760
all she ever wanted was to fight for the people&nbsp;
she loves. She finally gets her chance when she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:24.640
disguises herself in armor and rides with the&nbsp;
army, along with her new hobbit friend, Merry.

00:13:24.640 --> 00:13:29.760
Eowyn's time to shine comes when she faces off&nbsp;
against the dreaded Witch King on the battlefield.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:33.680
As the leader of the Nazgul taunts&nbsp;
that he cannot be killed by any man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:35.680
Eowyn removes her helmet, declaring:

00:13:36.560 --> 00:13:37.600
"I am no man."

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:42.560
She then offers the killing blow that&nbsp;
destroys the Witch King once and for all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.560 --> 00:13:45.520
and gives fans one of the most&nbsp;
victorious moments in the trilogy.

00:13:46.240 --> 00:13:50.320
"The Return of the King" features a&nbsp;
dramatic climax that culminates within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.320 --> 00:13:56.080
the perilous realm of Mordor. After traversing the&nbsp;
treacherous plains, escaping the clutches of orcs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.080 --> 00:14:01.360
and ascending the volcanic Mount Doom, Frodo and&nbsp;
Sam find the completion of their quest close at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:06.160
hand. All of their struggles and suffering will&nbsp;
soon come to a conclusion — one way or the other.

00:14:06.160 --> 00:14:11.200
As they enter into the heart of Mount Doom,&nbsp;
Frodo hesitates at the edge of the fiery abyss.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:16.240
Much to the horror of his loyal companion — and&nbsp;
the audience — Frodo has a sudden change of heart.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.240 --> 00:14:19.280
Instead of destroying the ring,&nbsp;
he decides to claim it as his own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.280 --> 00:14:23.280
placing it on his finger and making&nbsp;
himself invisible. All seems lost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:27.440
until the vengeful creature known as Gollum&nbsp;
interferes. Determined to get to the One Ring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.440 --> 00:14:31.680
Gollum attacks the invisible Frodo and&nbsp;
bites off the finger wearing the ring.

00:14:31.680 --> 00:14:35.760
As any "Lord of the Rings" fan will tell you,&nbsp;
Gollum inadvertently becomes the hero of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:40.400
story at that moment. Back in "The Fellowship of&nbsp;
the Ring," it was Gandalf himself that predicted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.400 --> 00:14:45.680
Gollum would have a big part to play. Tragically,&nbsp;
however, Gollum meets his demise when he plummets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.680 --> 00:14:50.480
into the lava pit below, still clinging to&nbsp;
his beloved treasure. The vision of Gollum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.480 --> 00:14:55.760
sinking into the fiery liquid, followed by the One&nbsp;
Ring, is one that will live forever in our minds.

00:14:56.640 --> 00:15:00.000
After the One Ring is destroyed in "The&nbsp;
Return of the King," the audience can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:03.680
finally breathe a collective sigh&nbsp;
of relief. Sauron is defeated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.680 --> 00:15:08.560
the remaining fellowship has been reunited,&nbsp;
and all is once again well in Middle-earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.560 --> 00:15:13.440
Now it's time for Aragorn to finally take&nbsp;
his place as the rightful King of Gondor.

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:18.480
The coronation scene is one of both celebration&nbsp;
and solemnity. Everyone has gathered to see the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:23.520
crowning of Aragorn, their heroic leader and&nbsp;
faithful friend. As the crowd of subjects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:28.640
bow and make way for their new king, Aragorn&nbsp;
reaches the line of the four hobbits: Frodo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:34.400
Sam, Pippin, and Merry. Yet when the halflings&nbsp;
begin bowing to him, Aragorn protests, insisting

00:15:34.400 --> 00:15:37.680
"You bow to no one."

00:15:37.680 --> 00:15:42.800
The new king then kneels before them and bows his&nbsp;
head, and the massive crowd follows his example.

00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:47.840
The sight of so many kneeling before the humble&nbsp;
hobbits is enough to make anyone gasp in awe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.840 --> 00:15:52.480
as well as bring a tear to your eye. These&nbsp;
four characters have come a very long way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.480 --> 00:15:56.640
from their simple life in the Shire. Each&nbsp;
of them has demonstrated his bravery and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.640 --> 00:16:01.360
resilience in the face of impossible&nbsp;
odds, and came out forever changed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.360 --> 00:16:05.360
This relevant moment reminds us of the most&nbsp;
important theme of "The Lord of the Rings"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.360 --> 00:16:10.400
trilogy: anyone — no matter their size or&nbsp;
stature — is capable of doing great things.

